WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.930
Welcome to this module of The Water We Swim In.

2
00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:05.810
This one is called Where it comes from. It asks

3
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:08.230
a simple question about the habits we bring to work.

4
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:09.610
Where did they come from?

5
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.260
And it starts in a church basement. A funeral lunch

6
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.960
in a small Minnesota town, with long tables, folding chairs,

7
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.820
coffee in a big urn and at least three kinds

8
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:20.320
of hotdish.

9
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.130
Nobody sent a sign-up sheet. People just knew what to

10
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:27.070
bring. I have been to that lunch more times than

11
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:27.510
I can count.

12
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.620
The module points out that hotdish has a history. The

13
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.040
Minnesota Historical Society's online encyclopedia traces the earliest known recipe

14
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.440
to a 1930 cookbook from a Lutheran church women's society

15
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:40.080
in Mankato.

16
00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.950
After World War Two it moved into home kitchens, served

17
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.190
at showers and funerals as a way to show care.

18
00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.290
So something that feels like just food grew out of

19
00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:53.430
particular churches and particular immigrants. The module asks us to

20
00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.070
look at our work habits the same way.

21
00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.940
It begins with a helpful idea. A culture is partly

22
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.820
a set of answers that earlier people worked out to

23
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:02.720
the problems they faced.

24
00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.570
And it is honest about where the story begins. It

25
00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:10.310
begins with land. The encyclopedia describes the state's origins as

26
00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:13.830
primarily about the taking of land from Native people. The

27
00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.390
settlement story that follows began on land that was taken.

28
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.640
Then come the settlers. By 1917, Germans were the state's

29
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.600
largest single ethnic group. Hundreds of thousands of Norwegians arrived,

30
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.040
often whole families who settled near one another because they

31
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.040
feared extreme isolation. And more Swedes settled in Minnesota than

32
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:33.900
in any other state.

33
00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.490
What struck me is the role of the church. It

34
00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:40.810
was school, social club and aid society, and later a

35
00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:41.910
social service agency.

36
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.100
That's a good way to see it. It helps explain

37
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.360
why so much help in Minnesota still flows through organizations.

38
00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.690
The module also describes a time when the majority culture

39
00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:55.630
learned to keep its head down. During World War One,

40
00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:59.890
a state commission watched German Americans closely. Swedish Lutheran churches

41
00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:03.790
moved to English services in the 1920s and 1930s, partly

42
00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.270
under pressure to Americanize.

43
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.480
And it is careful here. It asks whether some of

44
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:11.100
today's caution about standing out was learned in those years.

45
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.200
Then it says plainly that no source answers that question.

46
00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:18.390
Many people point to a Scandinavian source instead. In a

47
00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.110
2009 Minnesota Public Radio essay, the playwright Syl Jones tied

48
00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:25.110
Minnesota nice to the Jante law. Those are the unwritten

49
00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.130
rules of a fictional town in a 1930s novel, and

50
00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.830
they begin, "Don't think that you are special."

51
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.280
The module reminds us that is one writer's essay, not

52
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.200
a study. It is worth knowing because so many people

53
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:37.840
find it familiar.

54
00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:42.270
It also names who the usual story leaves out. The

55
00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.810
Dakota and Ojibwe people whose land this was. Black Minnesotans

56
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.870
of the Rondo neighborhood in Saint Paul and of north

57
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:51.790
Minneapolis. And Mexican farmworkers, about seven thousand of whom worked

58
00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:54.230
the sugar beet fields as early as 1928.

59
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.620
A culture's picture of itself is a selection. I keep

60
00:02:58.620 --> 00:02:59.660
coming back to that line.

61
00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.930
So where did Minnesota nice come from? A University of

62
00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.490
Saint Thomas sociologist, Lisa Waldner, told Minnesota Public Radio in

63
00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.950
2019 that there is no definitive documentation.

64
00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:16.800
But she offered two likely threads. Northern European settlers clustered

65
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.000
by group, so people lived side by side without mixing

66
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.780
much. In her words, they "co-existed, but you didn't intermingle."

67
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:27.810
The second thread is the farm. If your plow broke

68
00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:31.210
at harvest, you might need your neighbor. So politeness won

69
00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.130
out over hostility.

70
00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.360
Which sets up a real tension. There is a strong

71
00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.800
ethic of handling your own problems, right alongside a practical

72
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.120
dependence on the people nearby.

73
00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.650
Winter is part of it too. The humorist Howard Mohr

74
00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:49.870
connected Minnesota sociability to a stoicism born of outlasting brutal

75
00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:53.170
winters. The module is clear that this is humor from

76
00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:54.990
a close observer, not research.

77
00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.400
And a University of Minnesota researcher, Grace Vieth, has linked

78
00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.300
newcomers' isolation partly to long winters, when people stay with

79
00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:06.320
the friends they already have. That is worth remembering when

80
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:07.820
someone new joins your team in November.

81
00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:11.830
Then there are the cooperatives. In 1890 the Danish American

82
00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:14.510
community at Clarks Grove founded one of the state's first

83
00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:18.070
cooperative creameries. For decades it was also a community center,

84
00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:20.590
with talks, meetings and school recitals.

85
00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.760
By 1928, nearly half of all the cooperative creameries in

86
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.860
the United States were in Minnesota. Pooling resources, sharing profits

87
00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.180
and losses, and meeting to decide. Those habits have deep

88
00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:33.700
roots here.

89
00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:38.830
The module then turns to government. The political scientist Daniel

90
00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:43.370
Elazar called Minnesota the archetypical example of a moralistic political

91
00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:46.670
culture. Politics is a shared search for the good society,

92
00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:50.210
and government is a positive instrument for the general welfare.

93
00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.080
The module asks us to treat that as a mirror,

94
00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.320
not a label. It is one scholar's classification, and it

95
00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.800
describes a state's public life, not any person in it.

96
00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:05.010
I appreciated that Elazar named a tension that matters for

97
00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.590
a large agency like the Minnesota Department of Human Services.

98
00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.930
He said this culture has a major difficulty with bureaucracy.

99
00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:15.330
And where merit systems exist, they tend to be rigidly

100
00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:16.010
maintained.

101
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:20.680
The history behind it is real, though. The Farmer-Labor Party

102
00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:24.900
grew out of farmers and workers organizing. The 1967 state

103
00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.340
civil rights law passed with support from both parties. And

104
00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:32.260
the 1971 Minnesota Miracle shifted most school costs to the

105
00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:32.480
state.

106
00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:37.690
In 2024, about seventy-six percent of eligible Minnesotans voted, second

107
00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:40.930
in the nation. But the module also cites a 2025

108
00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:45.330
column arguing that Minnesota exceptionalism was a myth. Easy consensus

109
00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.730
is part of the inheritance, but not a promise.

110
00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.940
Then it names the values under the surface. Each one

111
00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.700
is a strength, and each one can hide something. Self-reliance

112
00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.980
comes first, with a twist. Help here is often organized

113
00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.860
through institutions, and given more easily than it is asked

114
00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:01.160
for.

115
00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.350
Then treating everyone the same. The question is whether fairness

116
00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:09.330
means identical treatment, or treatment that gets people to the

117
00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:09.810
same place.

118
00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:14.780
Modesty and privacy are next. A culture that keeps its

119
00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:17.920
own troubles private may find it awkward to ask others

120
00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:20.380
about theirs. And so much of our work depends on

121
00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:20.580
asking.

122
00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.490
Right. And last, consensus and trust in process. Trust in

123
00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.210
institutions is a strength. It can also be a blind

124
00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.110
spot with people whose own or family history gives them

125
00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:32.110
reason to trust less.

126
00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:35.780
The module closes with a point I want to underline.

127
00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:39.460
None of these values belongs to every Minnesotan. They are

128
00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:43.320
inherited defaults, and knowing their history lets you choose when

129
00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.340
to lean on them and when to set them aside.

130
00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.590
There is a good example in the module. A new

131
00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:50.830
colleague who came from a state agency in the South

132
00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:55.070
is puzzled. Nobody takes credit, a coworker turned down help

133
00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.710
twice, and a small change took four meetings.

134
00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:01.140
The easy answer is that this is just how professional

135
00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.740
teams work. But that treats local habits as the universal

136
00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:08.980
standard. A better answer names the roots, then asks how

137
00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:10.420
her old team handled these things.

138
00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.150
So here is one thing to try this week. Name

139
00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.030
one value you hold at work, like waiting for consensus

140
00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:19.590
or not asking for help. Then trace it to where

141
00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:23.030
you learned it. A church basement, a family farm, a

142
00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:23.530
first supervisor.

143
00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:27.060
Write the pair down where you will see it. And

144
00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:29.400
read the full module for the sources and the detail

145
00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.340
behind everything we talked about. Thanks for spending this time

146
00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.800
with us, and take good care this week.
